  --> G_ij = identity, so this basis IS Cartesian-orthonormal.
  G^1 = [1. 0. 0.]      (equals G_1 here because basis is orthonormal)
  G^2 = [0. 1. 0.]      (equals G_2)
  Duality check G^i . G_j (should equal Kronecker delta):
    G^1.G_1 = +1.0000     G^1.G_2 = +0.0000
    G^2.G_1 = +0.0000     G^2.G_2 = +1.0000

SPATIAL config -- stretched, tilted, non-planar:
  g_1 = [1.5   0.    0.225]    |g_1| = 1.5168
  g_2 = [0.    1.    0.275]    |g_2| = 1.0371
  g_3 = [-0.14314144 -0.26242597  0.95427624]    (unit normal)
  g_ij =
    [2.300625 0.061875]
    [0.061875 1.075625]
  --> g_ij is NOT identity. Off-diagonal g_12 != 0 means the
      basis is skewed; diagonal entries != 1 means the basis
      is stretched relative to unit parameter steps.
  g^1 = [ 0.65300702 -0.02504269  0.09106431]      (DIFFERS from g_1 because basis is non-orthonormal)
  g^2 = [-0.03756403  0.93113261  0.25042686]      (DIFFERS from g_2)
  Duality check g^i . g_j:
    g^1.g_1 = +1.0000     g^1.g_2 = +0.0000
    g^2.g_1 = +0.0000     g^2.g_2 = +1.0000

GREEN-LAGRANGE STRAIN at centre  (E_kl = 1/2 (g_kl - G_kl)):
  E_kl =
    [0.6503125 0.0309375]
    [0.0309375 0.0378125]
  E_11 = +0.6503    (xi-direction normal strain)
  E_22 = +0.0378    (eta-direction normal strain)
  E_12 = +0.0309    (shear strain)

  Interpretation: E_11 > 0 means the spatial element is longer
  per unit xi than the material element -- a stretch in that
  parameter direction. E_22, E_12 likewise.
  These are NOT Cartesian strains; they are components in the
  (G^k, G^l) basis. The same physical state expressed in a
  local Cartesian frame would have different numerical values.

